# Integration of Alloy Segregation and Surface CoO Hybridization in Carbon‐Encapsulated CoNiPt Alloy Catalyst for Superior Alkaline Hydrogen Evolution

## abstract
Constructing an efficient alkaline hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) catalyst with low platinum (Pt) consumption is crucial for the cost reduction of energy devices, such as electrolyzers. Herein, nanoflower‐like carbon‐encapsulated CoNiPt alloy catalysts with composition segregation are designed by pyrolyzing morphology‐controlled and Pt‐proportion‐tuned metal–organic frameworks (MOFs). The optimized catalyst containing 15% CoNiPt NFs (15%: Pt mass percentage, NFs: nanoflowers) exhibits outstanding alkaline HER performance with a low overpotential of 25 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm−2, far outperforming those of commercial Pt/C (47 mV) and the most advanced catalysts. Such superior activity originates from an integration of segregation alloy and Co‐O hybridization. The nanoflower‐like hierarchical structure guarantees the full exposure of segregation alloy sites. Density functional theory calculations suggest that the segregation alloy components not only promote water dissociation but also facilitate the hydrogen adsorption process, synergistically accelerating the kinetics of alkaline HER. In addition, the activity of alkaline HER is volcanically distributed with the surface oxygen content, mainly in the form of Co3dO2p hybridization, which is another reason for enhanced activity. This work provides feasible insights into the design of cost‐effective alkaline HER catalysts by coordinating kinetic reaction sites at segregation alloy and adjusting the appropriate oxygen content.

Constructing an efficient alkaline hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) catalyst with low platinum (Pt) consumption is crucial for the cost reduction of energy devices, such as electrolyzers. Herein, nanoflower-like carbon-encapsulated CoNiPt alloy catalysts with composition segregation are designed by pyrolyzing morphology-controlled and Pt-proportion-tuned metal–organic frameworks (MOFs). The optimized catalyst containing $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs 1 $175\%$ : Pt mass percentage, NFs: nanoflowers) exhibits outstanding alkaline HER performance with a low overpotential of $25~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{v}$ at a current density of $\mathsf{10m A c m}^{-2}$ , far outperforming those of commercial $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{C}\left(47\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\right)$ and the most advanced catalysts. Such superior activity originates from an integration of segregation alloy and Co-O hybridization. The nanoflower-like hierarchical structure guarantees the full exposure of segregation alloy sites. Density functional theory calculations suggest that the segregation alloy components not only promote water dissociation but also facilitate the hydrogen adsorption process, synergistically accelerating the kinetics of alkaline HER. In addition, the activity of alkaline HER is volcanically distributed with the surface oxygen content, mainly in the form of $\mathsf{C o}_{3\mathrm{d}}\mathsf{-O}_{2\mathsf{p}}$ hybridization, which is another reason for enhanced activity. This work provides feasible insights into the design of cost-effective alkaline HER catalysts by coordinating kinetic reaction sites at segregation alloy and adjusting the appropriate oxygen content.
## 1. Introduction
Given the energy shortage and environmental problems (such as atmospheric pollution and the greenhouse effect) caused by traditional fossil fuels,[1–6] it is urgent to switch to alternative and pollution-free renewable energy sources. Hydrogen energy is widely recognized as one of the most promising candidates for green energy sources due to its advantages of zero-pollutant emission and high energy density for power conversion devices.[7] An anion exchange membrane (AEM) electrolyzer driven by electricity is considered as a feasible strategy to produce large-scale and sustainable hydrogen. However, the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at the cathode is limited by the water dissociation progress (Volmer step: $\mathrm{H}_{2}O\ +\ e^{-}\ \rightarrow\ \mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ad}}\ +\ \mathrm{OH}^{-})$ and requires more energy to dissociate water molecules than under acidic conditions.[8–10] Therefore, it remains challenge to explore the construction of cost-effective and stable alkaline HER catalysts with excellent activity.[11–17] Platinum (Pt)-based catalyst is an excellent HER catalyst due to its suitable adsorption/desorption energy for H intermediates. Unfortunately, the scarcity of reserves dramatically increases the overall cost of energy facility production. Recently, it has been noted that earthabundant transition metals or transition metal oxide-based catalysts have lower energy barriers in the water dissociation step.[18] Nevertheless, the poor hydrogen adsorption energy makes its energy conversion efficiency difficult to compete with that of Ptbased catalysts.[19,20] Given their respective advantages, it is a feasible strategy to prepare alloy-based catalyst by introducing trace Pt into transition metal, which ensures high efficiency and reduces the cost. Compared with a single metal element, the strain/ligand/ensemble effect induced by metal alloy greatly promote catalytic activity by influencing the physicochemical properties, including geometry/electronic property and coordination environment.[21,22] Besides, the alloy nanoparticles need strong interaction with the carbon carrier to avoid the activity failure caused by agglomeration.
Recent studies demonstrated that carbon-encapsulated metal alloy structures, derived from metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), can efficiently improve the structural sturdiness of metal, thus enhancing its durability.[23–25] Hofmann-type MOFs are a special class of bimetallic materials with three-dimensional pillarlayered structures,[26–28] which attract wide attention in gas adsorption separation, catalysis, and magnetism because of their high specific surface areas, adjustable pore size, multimetallic active site, and simple synthesis method.[29–32] In contrast to the introduction of noble metal elements (Pt, Ru) into MOFs material by means of solution impregnation (physical absorption) or ligand modification (unsaturated coordination sites),[33] Hofmanntype MOFs perfectly avoid the difficulty of accurately controlling the loading quality and location of precious metals, because in Hofmann’s pillar-layered bimetallic structure, the precious metal elements (Pt or Pd) act as metallic nodes, allowing their quality and location to be precisely controlled at the atomic level. More importantly, the morphology of Hofmann-type MOFs can also be controllably regulated by optimizing the synthetic conditions. Therefore, the development of high-efficiency alkaline HER catalyst by pyrolyzing Hofmann-type MOFs with low Pt content and multiple catalytic active sites is of great significance in energy storage and conversion equipment.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and Structural Characterizations
The detailed synthesis process of the $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst is illustrated in Figure 1. First, a series of CoNiPt-MOF precursors with different morphologies and $\mathrm{Pt}$ contents were simply synthesized by regulating and controlling synthetic parameters (reaction time, surfactant concentration, and $\mathrm{Pt}$ content). Subsequently, the CoNiPt-MOF precursors were further thermally converted into carbon-encapsulated CoNiPt alloy catalysts. Finally, the carbonized black power was treated with an acid solution and transformed into carbon-encapsulated CoNiPt alloy catalysts with composition segregation. The surface morphology of catalysts was first examined by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM). Adjusting the synthetic strategy of CoNiPt-MOF precursors, three diverse micromorphologies were observed, such as aggregates, nanosheets, and nanoflowers (AGs, NSs, NFs) (Figure S1a–S4a, Supporting Information). Amongst, the NFs-like morphology composes of ample irregularly arranged nanosheets (Figure 2a). Subsequently, after pyrolysis and acid treatment, one can observe that a substantial change in the morphology of AGs and NSs evolves into the aggregates of particles and carbon nanotubes (Figure S1b–2b, Supporting Information). In contrast, the overall nanoflower morphology remains unchanged, while the surface becomes wrinkled and rough (Figure 2b; Figure S3b–4b, Supporting Information). This 3D nanoflower-like structure with abundant edge sites enables rapid pumping of liquid electrolyte onto the active catalytic sites in view of a large specific area and exposure of more metal alloy catalytic active sites.[34] The internal morphology characterization by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) manifests that they are multi-layer winding carbon-encapsulated nanoalloy particles structure (Figures S5–S8, Supporting Information), which can enhance the corrosion resistance of the catalytic interface.[35,36] Figure 2c–e demonstrates the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images of $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst. Figure 2c displays a clear lattice fringe with a distance of $0.18\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (200) plane of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.5}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.5}$ (PDF No. 04-008490). The lattice spacings of 0.19 and $0.22{\mathrm{nm}}$ are demonstrated in Figure 2e, which are classified as the (111) and (200) facets of $\mathrm{Co}_{0.25}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.25}\mathrm{Pt}_{0.5}$ (PDF No. 04-003-9441) along with a great deal of lattice mismatching or defects. This result of HRTEM spectra suggests the formation of the CoNiPt alloys and the existence of component segregation, which may significantly increase the electrocatalytic activity as a result of the synergy of component segregation in alloys. Furthermore, the energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mappings evince the distribution of C, N, O, Co, Ni, and $\mathrm{Pt}$ elements in the $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst (Figure 2f). Meanwhile, the heterogeneous signal strength of Co, Ni, and Pt was also observed, revealing the existence of different alloy phases.[37,38] EDS line scanning results further clearly proved the contents of Co, Ni, and Pt in the prepared catalysts (Figure $2\mathrm{g})$ . It was found that CoNi is dominant in certain regions, and Pt is dominant in other regions, named as CoNi-rich phase and Pt-rich phase, respectively. This result indicates that CoNi atoms were etched away from some regions after the acid treatment, while the Pt element remains unchanged and yields a Pt-rich phase. This also explains the formation of the lattice mismatching or surface defects in HRTEM images, indicating the presence of disorder/defects. To further study the crystalline of as-prepared catalysts by X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Figure S9, Supporting Information), all prepared catalysts manifest similar characteristic diffraction peaks. The diffraction peak located at $26.1^{\circ}$ is considered to be the (002) plane of graphite carbon.[39,40] The peaks at $43.3^{\circ}$ , $51.6^{\circ}$ , and $76.1^{\circ}$ are attributed to the CoNi-rich phase, while the additional diffraction peaks belong to the Pt-rich phase.[41] Interestingly, with the decrease in Pt content, the Pt-rich alloy phase gradually disappears. According to Vegard’s law, this phenomenon has resulted from the dealloying of Pt in the Pt-rich alloy phase.[42] Finally, the Pt mass content in the catalysts was measured by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) and X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS), as shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information). The result measured by ICP was in well agreement with that of XPS, suggesting that the Pt quality detected in the catalyst is accurate. The name of the catalyst is mainly based on the Pt quality detected by ICP.
## 2.2. Alkaline HER Performance Evaluation
The alkaline HER activity was evaluated by a three-electrode system (see the methods section for details). As illustrated in Figure 3a, the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of CoNiPt catalysts with different morphologies and Pt content were operated in $1.0\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ . Through the morphological modulation strategy, the HER activity of the $20\%$ CoNiPt NFs is superior to $20\%$ CoNiPt NSs, followed by $20\%$ CoNiPt AGs. Then, by optimizing Pt content, $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst exhibits remarkable HER activity with the lowest overpotential $(25\mathrm{mV})$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , far surpassing commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (47 mV) and other synthesized catalysts (Figure 3b). The results reveal that the increase of Pt doping content does not always boost the HER catalytic performance of catalysts. Therefore, we can design electrocatalysts with low Pt usage in a controllable way. Correspondingly, Tafel plots of all catalysts are used to analyze the rate-determining step (Figure 3c). The Tafel slope of $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs $(43\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ ) is lower than those of $20\%$ CoNiPt NFs $(44\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}})$ , $20\%$ CoNiPt NSs $(52\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ , $20\%$ CoNiPt AGs $(53\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ ), $4\%$ CoNiPt NFs $(86\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ ), and $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ $(68\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}),$ ). This result indicates that the electrochemical desorption of hydrogen is the rate-limiting step (Volmer–Heyrovsky step) in the prepared catalyst.[43] In addition, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) analysis was also performed to investigate the charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ of prepared catalysts (Figure 3d). The $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst possesses the smallest semicircle radius compared to other catalysts, indicating higher electronic conductivity, thus accelerating HER reaction kinetics. According to the equivalent circuit diagram (Table S1, Supporting Information), the solution resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ of all catalysts is about $6.5–9.7\Omega$ . The $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ for $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs is $6.75\Omega$ , which is lower than that of $20\%$ CoNiPt NFs $(7.68\Omega)$ , $20\%$ CoNiPt NSs $(11.1\ \Omega)$ , $20\%$ CoNiPt AGs $(14.4~\Omega)$ , and $4\%$ CoNiPt NFs $(46.4~\Omega)$ , further suggesting that $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst possesses fastest HER reaction kinetics rate. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves with different scan rates are shown in Figure S11 (Supporting Information), the $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst possesses a larger slope than other catalysts, illustrating that HER intrinsic activity is significantly better than other catalysts. Moreover, the long-term stability of $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs was measured by the chronopotentiometry (CP). The $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst exhibits excellent catalytic activity even after $24\mathrm{h}$ of stability test (Figure 3e). In addition, the morphology of the $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst after stability test has not changed obviously, and the nanoflower structure is still maintained (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The cost and efficiency of catalyst are the key parameters to determine whether a catalyst can be applied in practice. As a result, the LSV curves normalized by electrode areas and $\mathrm{Pt}$ mass were used to further evaluate the catalytic activity (Figure $^{3\mathrm{f},\mathrm{g})}$ . The $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst exhibits 9.43 A $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\mathrm{mg_{pt}}^{-1}$ current density at $100~\mathrm{{mV}}$ overpotential, which is three times higher than that of commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ catalyst (3.11 A $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\mathrm{mg_{Pt}}^{-1})$ . It is suggested that the $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst can be a potential candidate to replace commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts in the future. In conclusion, the $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst demonstrates outstanding alkaline HER performance, including small overpotential $(25~\mathrm{mV})$ and small Tafel slope $(43\mathrm{~mV~dec^{-1}},$ ), far surpassing the benchmark $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst and the state-of-the-art electrocatalysts reported in recent literature (Figure 3h; Table S2, Supporting Information).[44–50]
