## 2.4. DFT Analysis
The reaction steps of alkaline HER were operated by DFT calculation to understand the catalytic mechanism. As well known, the alkaline HER involves the dissociation of the water molecules in the Volmer step and the formation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the Heyrovsky step or Tafel step, the detailed steps are as follows
Volmer step $:{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}+{\mathrm{e}}^{-}\rightarrow{^{\ast}\mathrm{H}}+{\mathrm{OH}}^{-}$
Heyrovsky step $:{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}+{\mathrm{\Pi}}^{*}{\mathrm{H}}+{\mathrm{e}}^{-}\rightarrow{\mathrm{H}}_{2}+{\mathrm{OH}}^{-}$ or Tafel step : $\mathrm{^{*}H}+\mathrm{^{*}H}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}$
## 2.5. Quantitative Analysis of HER Activity and Co–O Hybridization
In addition, alkaline HER activity is also closely related to $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-O}$ hybridization in the catalyst based on XAS experimental results. However, the source of oxygen and the specific relationship between HER activity and $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-O}$ hybridization in the catalyst still need to be further quantified and explored. Reviewing the synthesis process of the catalysts, the oxygen atom in $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-O}$ hybridization originates from the drying in an ordinary oven step after pickling. Hence, a large batch of the $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst was scaled up for synthesis and further carbonized. The same acid treatment was performed after average mass, and then different drying methods (freeze drying, vacuum, oven) and drying times in the oven $(5,5.5,6,6.5,7,9,12\mathrm{h})$ were regulated and controlled. The PXRD patterns of synthesized catalysts with different drying methods and drying times in the oven are described in Figure 6a. From the PXRD patterns, the diffraction peaks corresponding to the CoNi-rich phase and Pt-rich phase can be clearly observed, indicating that the catalyst is successfully repeated and can be synthesized in large quantities. Then, the LSV curves of catalysts in $1.0\mathrm{~m~}$ KOH were performed to explore the influence of different drying processes on HER activity. Figure 6b evinces the current density of the oven- $_{\textrm{\scriptsize6h}}$ catalyst is higher than the vacuum-6 h and freeze-drying catalysts, suggesting that the HER activity of the catalyst treated by oven drying is better than that of the catalyst treated by vacuum drying and freeze drying. Figure 6c displays the LSV curves of catalysts with different drying times in the oven, which shows that different drying time in the oven has a significant effect on HER activity. Hence, the relationship between HER activity and O atom% content in catalysts was demonstrated in Figure 6d. The percentage of oxygen atom in the catalyst is calculated as the average of multiple SEM-EDS data (Table S3, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure 6d, the HER activity increased first and then decreased with the increase of drying time in the oven, while the O atom% content in the catalyst increased with drying time in the oven. The relationship between HER activity and O atom% in the catalysts was further illustrated in Figure 6e. The HER activity is volcanically distributed along with the O atom $\%$ in the catalyst, which indicates that an appropriate amount of oxygen content in the catalyst could significantly improve HER performance. Therefore, the prepared catalyst can be properly exposed to air, but if the exposure time is too long, the catalyst will be ineffective.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, the nanoflower-like N-dope carbon-encapsulated CoNiPt alloy structure with composition segregation exhibits excellent HER activity in alkaline media. Among them, the optimal $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst exhibited a lower overpotential $(25~\mathrm{mV})$ in alkaline HER at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and the normalized current density at $100~\mathrm{{mV}}$ overpotential $(9.43\mathrm{~A~cm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{mg_{Pt}}^{-1})$ is three times higher than that of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst (3.11 $\mathrm{\Large~A~cm^{-2}~m g_{P t}}^{-1},$ ), far exceeding that of the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ and reported catalysts. Nanoflower-like geometry not only promotes rapid charge transfer but also exposes abundant alloy sites with segregation. Calculation proved that these segregation alloys in catalyst collaboratively accelerate the dissociation of water and the recombination of hydrogen steps. In addition, the electrocatalytic activity is also related to the surficial oxygen content of segregation alloys, which mainly exist in the form of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-O}$ hybridization, showing a volcanic-type relationship. Hence, the significant enhancement of activity is ascribable to the synergistic effect of these factors. This work provides a new idea for designing efficient and stable electrocatalysts with low platinum $\left(\mathrm{Pt}\right)$ consumption. It also lays a preliminary foundation for the future application of energy storage and conversion devices.
## 4. Experimental Section
Synthesis of $20\%$ CoNiPt AGs MOF Precursor: 0.3 mmol $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $(87~\mathsf{m g})$ and 0.3 mmol Pyrazine $(24\ m g)$ were dissolved in a $40m L$ mixture solution $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O};\mathsf{M e O H}=\mathsf{\Omega}\mathsf{I};\mathsf{I})$ to form clarifying solution. Separately, 0.27 mmol ${\sf K}_{2}[{\sf N i}({\sf C N})_{4}]\cdot{\boldsymbol{x}}{\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ $(65~\mathsf{m g})$ and $0.03~\mathsf{m m o l}$ $K_{2}[\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{C N})_{4}]\cdot3\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $(72.9~\mathsf{m g})$ were dissolved in $70~\mathsf{m L}$ deionized water and were dropped into the above clarifying solution. The reaction was stirred overnight and was centrifuged and washed with the water and methanol mixture three times. The pink precipitate was dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $2\mathfrak{h}$ .
Synthesis of $20\%$ CoNiPt NSs MOF Precursor: The synthetic conditions were the same as for $20\%$ CoNiPt AGs MOF except that the reaction time was controlled to $2\mathfrak{h}$ .
Synthesis of $20\%$ CoNiPt NFs MOF Precursor: 0.3 mmol $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $\{87\:\mathrm{mg}\}$ ), 0.3 mmol Pyrazine $(24~\mathsf{m g})$ , and PVP ( $(700\mathrm{mg})$ were dissolved in $80~\mathsf{m L}$ mixture solution $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O};\mathsf{M e O H}=\mathsf{I};\mathsf{I})$ ) to form clarifying solution. Separately, 0.27 mmol ${\sf K}_{2}[{\sf N i}({\sf C N})_{4}]\cdot{\boldsymbol{x}}{\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ $(65~\mathsf{m g})$ and $0.03~\mathsf{m m o l}$ ${\sf K}_{2}[\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{C N})_{4}]\cdot3{\sf H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ $(72.9~\mathsf{m g})$ were dissolved in $70~\mathsf{m L}$ deionized water and were dropped into the above clarifying solution. The reaction stirred for $2\mathfrak{h}$ and was centrifuged and washed by the water and methanol mixture three times. The pink precipitate was dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $2\mathfrak{h}$ .
Synthesis of $15\%$ or $4\%$ CoNiPt NFs MOF Precursor: The synthetic conditions were the same as $20\%$ CoNiPt NFs MOF except for the content of $K_{2}[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{C N})_{4}]{\cdot}x\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ ( $68~\mathsf{m g}$ or $72\ m g$ ) and ${\sf K}_{2}[\mathsf{P t}(\mathsf{C N})_{4}]\cdot3{\sf H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ ( $6~\mathrm{mg}$ or 1.3 mg).
Preparation of Series of CoNiPt Catalysts: All as-prepared MOF precursors were put in ${\mathsf{A l}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ crucible, heated to $800^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with a heating rate of $5^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ and kept for $2h$ under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere in a tube furnace, respectively. Then, the samples were cooled at room temperature and immersed in a $3\%H C l$ aqueous solution overnight. Subsequently, the samples were centrifuged and washed with water. The black precipitate was dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in a drying oven.
Preparation of Catalysts for HER Activity-O Content Experiment: A large batch of the $15\%$ CoNiPt NFs catalyst (2 g) was synthesized and carbonized. Followed by the same acid treatment after average mass: after weighing $60~\mathsf{m g}$ of carbonized powder into $5~\mathrm{mL}$ glass vial and adding $4m L3\%$ HCl aqueous solution immersed overnight, the resulted product was filtered and washed with deionized water until neutral. Then, different drying methods (freeze-drying, vacuum, oven) and drying time in the oven (5, 5.5, 6, 6.5, 7, 9, 12 h) were adjusted and controlled to obtain different catalysts.
Characterizations: The XRD data were measured on a Bruker AXS D8 Advance X-ray powder diffractometer equipped with a sealed $\mathsf{C u}$ tube $(\lambda=7.5406\mathring{\mathsf{A}}_{,}^{}$ ) operating at $40~\mathsf{k V}$ and $40~\mathsf{m A}$ . The Pt content in the catalysts was detected on ICP-OES (Agilent 7700 series). The XPS were collected on Thermo Scientific K-Alpha, and binding energies were calibrated by the C 1s peak at $284.8\mathsf{e V}.$ The HRTEM images were performed on an FEI Tecnai G2 F20 transmission electron microscope. The EDS mapping and line scanning images were obtained on OXFORD X-max 80T transmission electron microscope. The EXAFS spectra at the Pt $\mathsf{L}_{3}$ -edge, Ni K-edge, and Co K-edge were measured at beamline BL11B of the Shanghai Synchrotron Radiation Facility (SSRF) in transmission mode. The XANES spectra at C K-edge, N K-edge, O K-edge, Co K-edge, and Ni K-edge were collected at beamline BL11U, MCD-A, and MCD-B (Soochow Beamline for Energy Materials) in the National Synchrotron Radiation Laboratory (NSRL) in Hefei, China, respectively.
Electrochemical Measurements: Electrochemical tests were measured in a German Zahner electrochemical station with a three-electrode system. The working electrode was made as follows: 2 mg of catalyst was dispersed in $960~\upmu\upiota$ IPA and $40\upmu\up L$ Nafion solution under ultrasonication for $0.5\ h$ . $25~\upmu\upiota$ catalyst ink was dropped into the polished glass carbon disk electrode (diameter: $5\:\mathsf{m m}$ ) and dried at room temperature, and the loading of catalyst on the electrode was $0.254\mathrm{~mg~cm}^{-2}$ . The ${\sf A g/A g C l}$ and the graphite rod electrode were used as the reference and counter electrodes, respectively. The potential of the $\sf A g/A g C l$ electrode was calibrated before use. For HER measurements in ${\sf N}_{2}$ -saturated ${\mathsf{1.0~w~K O H}}$ , LSV curves were tested at a scan rate of $5\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ . CP was conducted at a current density of $\mathsf{10\ m A c m^{-2}}$ for $24\mathsf{h}$ for a stability test. EIS was set from 0.1 to $100000\mathsf{H z}$ at $0.6\mathsf{V}$ bias voltage and $\boldsymbol{{\mathsf{10}}}\boldsymbol{{\mathsf{m}}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{V}}$ amplitude. All LSV curves were corrected with iR-compensation. The potentials of the reversible hydrogen electrode were calculated by the Nernst equation: E $(V\lor s\mathsf{R H E})=E$ (V vs Ag/AgCl) $+0.797+0.059\mathsf{p H}$ .
DFT Calculation: Based on the XRD result, the FCC $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{N i}$ (mp1008349) and $\mathsf{C o P t}_{3}$ (mp-922) crystal structures were used from the Material Project. The surface was built by building supercells and cleaving (200) and (111) lattice planes of ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{N i}}$ and $\mathsf{C o P t}_{3}$ , respectively. Five-layer interface systems were thereafter obtained by adding $\dot{7}0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ vacuum layers at both upper and lower surfaces. To consider the ternary alloy synthesized during the experiment, one Pt and one Ni atom were added at the $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{N i}$ (200) and $\mathsf{C o P t}_{3}$ (111) surfaces, respectively. ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{N i}}$ (200) system contains a total of 90 metal atoms while the number was 80 for $\mathsf{C o P t}_{3}$ (111). The DFT calculations were performed with the Quantum Espresso package.[70] The Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE) functional within the GGA approximation was adopted in combination with the ultrasoft pseudopotential.[71] The plane-wave cutoff was set to be $544\ \mathrm{eV}$ (40 a.u.) for all the calculations and the Brillouin zone was sampled based on the Monkhorst–Pack scheme with a $2\times2\times1~\mathrm{k}$ -mesh.[72] The Van der Waals interaction was corrected with the Grimme-D3 method.[73] The climbing images nudged elastic band method was used to obtain the transition state with eight images.
